# Cursor Rules — Personal Site + Multi-App Monorepo

## Project Overview
This is a Turborepo monorepo containing a personal website (the "shell") plus
multiple independent applications ("apps"). Apps may be deployed either as:
  - subpaths of the main domain (e.g. example.com/app-name), or
  - standalone subdomains (e.g. app-name.example.com)
depending on the app. Deployment target is decided per-app, not globally —
never assume one routing strategy applies to all apps.

Apps are functionally INDEPENDENT: they do not share state, auth, or business
logic with each other. The only thing shared across apps is presentation:
UI components, design tokens, and the visual "shell" (header/footer/nav/theme).
Do not introduce cross-app state, shared stores, or shared auth sessions
unless explicitly asked — this is a deliberate architectural boundary, not
an oversight.

## Monorepo Structure
- `apps/web` — the main personal website / shell (marketing pages, home, nav
  into other apps).
- `apps/<app-name>` — one folder per application. Each is a self-contained
  Next.js app with its own routing, own local state, own data layer.
- `packages/ui` — shared, presentational-only component library (buttons,
  layout primitives, header/footer, theme provider, design tokens). No
  business logic. No app-specific state. No data fetching.
- `packages/config` — shared `tsconfig.base.json`, ESLint config, Tailwind
  config/preset, shared constants (e.g. brand colors, fonts).
- `packages/types` (optional, create if needed) — shared TypeScript types
  that are genuinely cross-cutting (e.g. shared UI prop types), NOT app
  domain models.

When adding a new app: scaffold it under `apps/<name>`, wire it into
`turbo.json` pipelines, give it its own `package.json`, and only import from
`packages/ui` and `packages/config`. Never import one app's code from
another app.

## Language & Tooling
- TypeScript everywhere, `strict: true`. No `any` unless justified with a
  comment explaining why; prefer `unknown` + narrowing.
- Framework: Next.js (App Router) for `apps/web` and every app in `apps/*`,
  for consistency and easy subpath/subdomain routing.
- Package manager: pnpm workspaces + Turborepo for task orchestration/caching.
- Styling: Tailwind CSS, consuming the shared preset from `packages/config`.
  Component-level styles stay colocated with components.
- Linting/formatting: shared ESLint + Prettier config from `packages/config`;
  do not create per-app lint configs that diverge from the shared base
  without a clear reason.

## Routing & Deployment Conventions
- Subpath apps: handled via Next.js rewrites/middleware in `apps/web` (or a
  reverse proxy at the infra layer) routing `/app-name/*` to the app's
  build output. Keep this routing logic centralized and documented, not
  duplicated per app.
- Subdomain apps: deployed as fully separate Next.js projects/deployments
  with their own domain config. They still consume `packages/ui` and
  `packages/config` for visual consistency, but are otherwise independent
  deployables — don't couple their build to `apps/web`.
- Never hardcode absolute domains inside `packages/ui`. Shell components
  take navigation targets (app name, path/URL) as props/config, not
  hardcoded links.

## Shared UI Package Rules (`packages/ui`)
- Presentational only. No `useEffect` data fetching, no global state, no
  auth checks. If a component needs data, it takes props — the consuming
  app supplies them.
- No app-specific business logic or copy leaks into `packages/ui`. Generic
  names only (e.g. `PrimaryButton`, not `SignupButton`).
- Every component should be usable identically whether rendered inside
  `apps/web` or any `apps/<name>` — treat it as a published design system.
- Export via a single index/barrel file per component group; keep the
  public API of the package intentional and minimal.

## State Management
- Each app manages its own local state independently. Default to React
  state/Context for simple cases; introduce a lightweight library (Zustand
  is the default recommendation) only within an app when local state grows
  complex — do not add a shared cross-app store.
- Do not build a shared auth/session layer across apps unless a future
  requirement explicitly calls for it. If that requirement appears, treat
  it as a deliberate architecture change, not an incremental addition —
  flag it before implementing.

## Code Style
- Functional components, no class components.
- Named exports for components; default export only for Next.js
  page/layout files where required by convention.
- Co-locate a component's types with the component unless the type is
  genuinely shared (then it goes in `packages/ui` or `packages/types`).
- Prefer composition over prop-drilling deep component trees; extract
  smaller components rather than adding boolean "mode" props.
- Write self-documenting code; add comments only for non-obvious "why",
  not "what".

## What NOT to do
- Don't import directly between `apps/*` folders.
- Don't put business logic, fetch calls, or environment-specific code
  inside `packages/ui`.
- Don't assume all apps share the same deployment/routing strategy —
  check per-app config before writing routing code.
- Don't introduce a shared global state/auth solution without explicit
  sign-off, even if it seems convenient for a new feature.
